  "details": "What I wanted/expected to do:\n\n```\n\ndict[(int)state] = (int)state;\n\n```\n\nThe variable `state` is an Enum I saved, for example `MyEnum.WHATEVER`.\n\nThe result is the following error:\n\n```\n\nCannot retrieve heap variable of type 'Int32' as type 'DataToken'\n\n```\n\nMore cases where I confirmed that it doesn't work:\n\n```\n\ndict[(int)state] = \"whatever\";\n\ndict[\"whatever\"] = (int)state;\n\ndict.TryGetValue((int)state), out DataToken value)\n\n```\n\nWorkaround:\n\nInitializing the int as a DataToken instance seems to work:\n\n```\n\nnew DataToken((int)fromState)\n\n```\n\nAlso, giving the enums directly seems to work too (not just as a variable):\n\n```\n\ndict[(int)MyEnum.WHATEVER] = \"whatever\";\n\n```\n\nIt would be really cool if I could just use an enum, either as key or value, without needing to cast or do anything special.",
